          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The passage tells the reader explicitly what Holl's book does and what it argues: it gathers radiocarbon dates, faunal assemblages, and pottery sequences from regional excavations, and it argues that the standard story of a single southward wave of farmers obscures a more variable picture in which communities combined herding, fishing, and cereal cultivation in locally distinct ways. The closing sentence states the contribution as replacing a single migration story with a local, variable account. Choice A captures both the evidentiary basis and the central claim at the right level of generalization.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: surface match — Xiamen University and the Cameroon and Mali fieldwork are both lifted from the biographical aside in the passage, but a fact about where the author teaches is not the main idea of the book the passage describes. Treating a biographical detail as the main idea mistakes setup for substance.\n- Choice C: inverse — this reverses Holl's argument. The passage says he treats the single-migration narrative as having \"obscured\" the more local picture and that his contribution is to \"replace\" it; choosing C turns rejection of the standard story into broad acceptance of it.\n- Choice D: scope shift — pottery sequences are mentioned as one of three lines of evidence, alongside radiocarbon dates and animal-bone assemblages. Promoting one item from a list to \"the single most important line of evidence\" overreads the passage and treats a sub-element as the main claim.",
