          "difficulty": "medium",
          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Working along the cliffs of Lyme Regis on the southern coast of England in the early nineteenth century, Mary Anning extracted, prepared, and identified some of the first articulated ichthyosaur and plesiosaur skeletons known to science. Her finds reshaped contemporary debates about whether species could go extinct, and the geologists William Buckland and Henry De la Beche relied heavily on her work in their published descriptions. At the time, however, women were excluded from membership in the Geological Society of London and from formal authorship of scientific papers. Anning's specimens therefore moved into the published record largely through the writings of male colleagues who acknowledged her by name unevenly: some named her in print and explained her preparation methods, while others identified her only as a local collector or omitted her contribution altogether. Twentieth- and twenty-first-century historians of science have reread the original notebooks and correspondence of those colleagues to recover, fossil by fossil, the role she actually played.",
