          "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The passage builds an argument across three moves: it first establishes Anning's scientific contribution (articulated ichthyosaur and plesiosaur skeletons that reshaped debates about extinction), then names the institutional barrier she faced (exclusion from the Geological Society and from formal authorship), and finally identifies the consequence — that her finds entered the published record through male colleagues whose acknowledgment of her was uneven — and notes the recent historiographical work that has tried to reconstruct her actual role from notebooks and correspondence. Choice C integrates all three moves and lands on the passage's overall point.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: scope shift — Buckland and De la Beche are mentioned as scientists who relied on her work, but the passage does not single them out as more reliable acknowledgers than others; it simply names them as users of her specimens. Treating a sub-claim about two named geologists as the main idea narrows the passage to a part of its setup.\n- Choice B: inverse — the passage says Anning's finds \"reshaped\" debates about extinction, not that they \"settled\" the debate within a few years. Choosing C inflates the historical claim from contributing to a debate into resolving it, which the passage does not assert.\n- Choice D: surface match — the passage explicitly says women were excluded from formal authorship of scientific papers, which is the opposite of D's claim that she authored more papers than any contemporary. D inverts the institutional fact the passage spends several sentences establishing.",
