          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Linda Nochlin's 1971 essay \"Why Have There Been No Great Women Artists?\" is often misremembered as a survey of overlooked women painters and sculptors. The essay does mention specific figures, but its central work is different. Nochlin, who was then at Vassar College, used the question in her title as a polemical opening to redirect the inquiry: rather than searching the historical record for women whose names belong on a list of \"great\" artists, she argued, scholars should examine the institutional conditions that made it possible to be classed as a great artist in the first place — access to academy training, to anatomy classes from which women were excluded, to studio apprenticeships, to public commissions, and to the critical infrastructure that confers reputation. The essay's lasting influence, the art historian Anne D'Alleva and others have suggested, owes less to any specific artist Nochlin recovered than to the way it reframed the question itself.",
