          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "Social epidemiologist Camara Phyllis Jones, a former president of the American Public Health Association, has long argued that population-health research benefits when its conceptual frameworks are made measurable rather than rhetorical. In a widely cited gardener's-tale article, she sets out three levels at which racism can act on health: institutionalized (rules and resources distributed unevenly across groups), personally mediated (differential treatment in everyday encounters), and internalized (the absorption of negative ascriptions about one's own group). Each level, she points out, leaves distinct traces in survey data and in administrative records, so each can be tracked, year over year, by indicators specific to it. Because all three levels operate on the same population at the same time, Jones's framework implies that researchers who collect data sensitive to only one of the three levels will ______",
          "question": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "id": "A",
              "text": "tend to underestimate the cumulative health burden the framework predicts at the population level."
            },
            {
              "id": "B",
              "text": "be unable to detect any health disparity at all in the population they study."
            },
            {
              "id": "C",
              "text": "find that the level they focus on accounts for nearly all of the observed health disparity in their data."
            },
            {
              "id": "D",
              "text": "demonstrate that institutionalized racism is the single most important driver of measurable health outcomes."
            }
          ],
          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises: (1) Jones's framework distinguishes three levels at which racism acts on health (institutionalized, personally mediated, and internalized), each leaving distinct traces in different kinds of records; and (2) all three levels act on the same population at the same time. Because the levels are simultaneous and additive, a study sensitive to only one level captures the trace from that one level but misses the contributions from the other two. The minimum inference the premises license is therefore that such studies will tend to underestimate the cumulative health burden Jones's framework predicts. Choice A states exactly this measured conclusion.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C: inverse — B claims that single-level studies will find their level accounts for nearly all of the disparity, which reverses the implication of the passage. If three levels act simultaneously, an instrument sensitive to one of them captures only its piece of the total; it cannot, on the framework's own logic, explain nearly the whole effect.\n- Choice B: scope shift — C overstates the conclusion. The premises imply underestimation of the cumulative burden, not the disappearance of any detectable disparity. A study sensitive to one of three contributing levels would still pick up the trace from that level; the claim is about magnitude, not about absence.\n- Choice D: surface match — D singles out institutionalized racism as the most important driver. The passage describes three levels and explicitly says all three operate on the same population at the same time, with no claim that one of the three carries the greatest weight. Promoting one named level to a top-driver claim is unsupported by the premises; it picks up a topical noun without matching the logical structure.",
