          "explanation": "The passage sets up a sufficient-versus-necessary distinction. Premise (1): OMZ specialists persist within OMZ cores and depend on the conditions there. Premise (2): OMZs are projected to expand. Premise (3): Levin explicitly stops short of saying expansion is uniformly bad — the expansion enlarges the very habitat the specialists require. Premise (4): the actual change in their populations will turn on whether the conditions inside the expanded zone remain within the specialists' tolerance range. The minimum inference these premises force is conditional, not directional: habitat-area increase by itself is consistent with population growth, decline, or no change, depending on whether the conditions inside the new area stay within the specialists' tolerance. Choice C states exactly this licensed conditional conclusion.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D: surface match — B is the strongest distractor and the load-bearing trap at this difficulty. It is consistent with one piece of the passage (more habitat could help the specialists), but it is not licensed by the premises. The passage explicitly hedges on the outcome and ties it to whether the conditions remain within the tolerance range; B promotes a possibility into a near-certain proportional expansion, which the premises do not support.\n- Choice A: inverse — C contradicts the passage. Levin says explicitly that the specialists \"persist within OMZ cores and depend on the chemical conditions there.\" Their persistence is part of the setup, so a claim that they will inevitably collapse reverses the premise.\n- Choice B: scope shift — D abandons the question the stem asks (the consequences of expansion for the specialists) and shifts to challenging the projection itself. Nothing in the passage casts doubt on the projection of OMZ expansion; the premises take the projection as given and ask what the consequences for specialists will be.",
