          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "Harriet Jacobs's 1861 narrative *Incidents in the Life of a Slave Girl*, published under the pseudonym Linda Brent, is in part an account of her seven-year concealment in a small attic crawl space in her grandmother's house in Edenton, North Carolina, after she escaped from her enslaver. A literary scholar argues that Jacobs uses the constrained physical conditions of the crawl space not as a passive setting but as a vantage point: the cramped space organizes what the narrator can see, hear, and learn, and the narrative repeatedly returns to that vantage when establishing what she knows about her children and about the search being conducted for her.",
