          "explanation": "The historian's claim is narrow and specific: the period in which the rate dropped fastest in absolute terms is not the one popular histories tend to highlight, and the supporting evidence must focus on the change between adjacent measurement points rather than on the rate at any single point. To support that claim, the choice must compare the decade-on-decade decreases shown in the rightmost column and identify the largest of them. Choice A does exactly that: 38 deaths per 1,000 between 1920 and 1940 is the largest single-interval decrease in the table, exceeding the 21-deaths drop between 1940 and 1960 and the 15-deaths drop between 1900 and 1920. That cross-interval comparison is what licenses the historian's claim.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D is a SCOPE SHIFT: it cites the highest absolute level (100 deaths per 1,000 in 1900) as if a high level of mortality at one point in time implied that the largest reduction occurred there. The claim is about between-interval declines, not about absolute levels at a single point; the table shows the 1900-1920 drop was only 15 deaths per 1,000, not the largest.\n- Choice C is an INVERSE: it points to the smallest single-interval decline shown (the 6-deaths drop from 1980 to 2000) and treats it as if it were the largest. The 2000 rate is indeed the lowest of the years shown, but that fact concerns the level at the endpoint, not the size of the most recent decline; the most recent interval is in fact the smallest decline in the table.\n- Choice B is a SURFACE MATCH on the spread of the data: the average across five intervals is mentioned, but the claim is about the largest single-interval decline, not about the typical decline. D also misreads the spread — the values (15, 38, 21, 13, 6) do not cluster closely around any single number.",
