          "correctAnswer": "C",
          "explanation": "The claim is specific: the narrator presents passing not as a single decisive choice but as a habit of routine small adjustments — of speech, of company kept, of stories told about himself — that shifts depending on the audience he expects to encounter on a given day. Choice C illustrates exactly that pattern. The narrator describes how, on days when he lunches with one set of men, his voice grows lower and his Southern turns of speech \"quietly absented themselves,\" while on days when he goes uptown to play, those same turns return and pull a story or two from his boyhood along with them. Both halves of the claim — small adjustments of speech and of stories told, calibrated to the day's audience — appear in a single quotation.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: surface match — A talks about the narrator's mother and his early education, which anchors the topic of family background, but it does not show the narrator making any audience-keyed adjustments. The claim concerns adult social practice, not childhood biography.\n- Choice D: scope shift — C captures the language of decisive transition (\"whatever life I had been living up to that hour was now closed\"), which sounds like a watershed moment and so resembles a single decisive choice. That is the opposite of the routine small-adjustments claim the critic is making; if anything, C illustrates the framing the critic is arguing against.\n- Choice B: inverse — D narrates a concert evening with no reference to the narrator's identity, audience-keyed speech, or self-presentation; it draws attention to the lamps and the piano rather than to any practice of passing. The detail is vivid but unrelated to the claim, which the quotation must illustrate to count as evidence.",
