          "passage": "Most large-scale drug-discovery programs in the mid-twentieth century relied on screening enormous libraries of synthetic compounds against pathogens or tumor cell lines. The biochemist and pharmacologist Gertrude B. Elion, working at Burroughs Wellcome alongside George Hitchings, took a different route: she and her colleagues studied the underlying biochemistry of nucleic-acid metabolism in bacteria, parasites, and human cells in order to predict which small molecules might selectively block pathogen pathways while leaving the patient's own cells intact. ______ rather than discovering useful drugs by serendipity, Elion's program produced 6-mercaptopurine for childhood leukemia, allopurinol for gout, and acyclovir for herpes simplex virus from a deliberate, mechanism-based design strategy.",
