          "explanation": "The first half of the passage describes the methodological choice Elion made: rather than screening enormous libraries by trial, she and her colleagues studied the biochemistry of nucleic-acid metabolism so that they could predict which compounds would selectively block pathogen pathways. The sentence after the blank then names what that approach actually produced — three specific, named drugs — and characterizes them as the products of a \"deliberate, mechanism-based design strategy.\" The logical relation between the two sentences is consequence: because of the mechanism-based approach, those drugs followed. \"As a result,\" is the standard CB transition for that direct causal relation and is the correct choice (C).\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (\"However,\"): inverse — \"However,\" signals reversal of expectation, but the second sentence does not reverse the first; it confirms it by naming the drugs the mechanism-based approach yielded. Choosing \"However,\" would imply that the named drugs ran counter to what the strategy was supposed to deliver, which is the opposite of the relation in the passage.\n- Choice D (\"In addition,\"): scope shift — \"In addition,\" signals that more information is being added on the same line of thought, treating the second sentence as a parallel further fact. But the second sentence is not a further parallel fact about Elion's strategy; it is the consequence of that strategy. Adding by accumulation rather than reading by causation misses the actual logical move.\n- Choice A (\"Meanwhile,\"): surface match — \"Meanwhile,\" is a temporal connector that signals that something is happening at the same time as something else. The passage does not stage two simultaneous events; it traces a strategy and the outcomes that followed from it. \"Meanwhile,\" picks up the temporal vocabulary of mid-twentieth-century drug discovery without matching the cause-and-result relation between the two sentences.",
