          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B, \"warranted.\" The passage states that the depth and stride of the Laetoli prints supplied the kind of evidence on which Leakey rested a fairly strong inference about hominin gait. The blank therefore needs a verb meaning to provide adequate grounds for or to justify a conclusion. \"Warranted\" fits exactly: it captures the relation between physical evidence (depth and stride of prints) and a defended scientific claim (upright bipedal walking) without overstating it as proof.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: scope shift — \"permitted\" is in the right general region (it concerns the relation of evidence to a conclusion), but the wrong specificity. \"Permitted the conclusion\" implies that the conclusion is merely allowed, not actually supported by the evidence; the surrounding clause attributes the conclusion to Leakey as her argument, not as a possibility she happened to consider compatible with the data.\n- Choice C: inverse — \"challenged\" reverses the logical direction. The passage says the depth and stride of the prints supported Leakey's conclusion about upright walking, not that they cast doubt on it. Choosing \"challenged\" turns supporting evidence into evidence against the same claim.\n- Choice D: surface match — \"advertised\" is sometimes used loosely to mean \"made evident,\" so it can sound like a near-synonym for \"supported.\" But it is not a verb scientific writing uses for the relation between physical evidence and a conclusion; it picks up a vaguely communicative register without matching the actual argumentative work the sentence describes.",
