          "correctAnswer": "D",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is D, \"against.\" The sentence describes a methodological practice: Gordon-Reed reads white-authored records that were never designed to capture the perspectives of enslaved people, and she draws inferences from precisely the gaps, omissions, and inconsistencies those records contain. To read a document \"against\" its silences is to read in tension with what the document leaves out, to position one's reading relative to what is unsaid. The phrase \"reading against the silences\" is a multi-word collocation in archive-based historical writing that names exactly this kind of contrarian, gap-attentive interpretation, and it matches the explanatory clause that follows (\"she pays close attention to what is omitted, to what is named only in passing, and to the inconsistencies between an enslaver's stated and actual practice\").\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"in spite of\"): scope shift — \"in spite of\" suggests that the silences of the records are an obstacle Gordon-Reed must work around, treating them as an unfortunate limitation. But the explanatory clause shows she does not work around the silences; she works through them and treats the absences as evidence in their own right. The phrase therefore captures only the contrastive register, not the active reading-with-the-gap method.\n- Choice C (\"in light of\"): inverse — \"in light of the silences\" would mean reading the documents under the guidance of, or in deference to, what they leave out, as if the silences supplied a stable interpretive frame. The sentence describes the opposite move: Gordon-Reed reads in tension with the silences, mining the gaps rather than yielding to them.\n- Choice B (\"on behalf of\"): surface match — \"on behalf of\" picks up the moral register of recovering an enslaved family's history, as if Gordon-Reed reads the records to speak for the silenced. But the sentence is making a methodological claim, not a representational one; \"reading on behalf of the silences\" is not a method, and the explanatory clause names a method (paying attention to omissions and inconsistencies) rather than an act of advocacy.",
