          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Edgar Lee Masters's 1915 collection *Spoon River Anthology*. Each of the book's 244 short poems is spoken from beyond the grave by a former resident of the fictional Illinois town of Spoon River. The poem below is in the voice of \"Lucinda Matlock.\"\n\nI went to the dances at Chandlerville,\nand played snap-out at Winchester.\nOne time we changed partners,\ndriving home in the moonlight of middle June,\nand then I found Davis.\nWe were married and lived together for seventy years,\nenjoying, working, raising the twelve children,\neight of whom we lost\nere I had reached the age of sixty.\nI spun, I wove, I kept the house, I nursed the sick,\nI made the garden, and for holiday\nrambled over the fields where sang the larks,\nand by Spoon River gathering many a shell,\nand many a flower and medicinal weed —\nshouting to the wooded hills, singing to the green valleys.\nAt ninety-six I had lived enough, that is all,\nand passed to a sweet repose.\nWhat is this I hear of sorrow and weariness,\nanger, discontent and drooping hopes?\nDegenerate sons and daughters,\nLife is too strong for you —\nIt takes life to love Life.",
          "question": "Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?",
