          "band": 3,
          "domain": "craft-and-structure",
          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Charles W. Chesnutt's 1899 collection *The Conjure Woman*. The narrator, a Northern white man named John, has just bought a former vineyard in eastern North Carolina, which he intends to bring back into cultivation. Old Julius, who has lived on the place all his life, sits with John and his wife under a tree and begins, unprompted, to talk about it.\n\nThe vineyard, after a year and a half of careful pruning, was beginning to show what it could do. Old Julius, however, did not seem inclined to celebrate. He had taken his usual seat on the second step of the porch, with a battered straw hat in his lap, and after a long preamble of throat-clearing and looking at the sky, he remarked that there had been some quare doings on the place when he was a boy. *I had learned, by now, that any prefatory remark of this kind from Julius signaled a story; that the story, however meandering it might appear at the outset, would in time arrive at a particular grapevine, mule, or stretch of bottomland; and that the moral of it, if such a thing were retrievable, would in some way bear on a decision my wife or I had recently been heard to discuss.* I therefore lit my pipe, leaned back, and arranged my face for the kind of attention that politeness, but not necessarily belief, required.",
