          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Text 2 does not contest the empirical claim that English can be bent to local rhythms; it accepts the practice Achebe describes and shifts the argument to its institutional cost. Ngugi's own framing makes the move explicit: continuing to write in English, even an English inflected for African readers, \"leaves intact the institutional conditions that placed English at the center of literary recognition in the first place — the metropolitan publishers, the prizes, the curricula.\" His own decision to write in Gikuyu and translate afterward is offered as a deliberate intervention in the contemporary geography of recognition, not as a denial that bending English is possible. Choice B captures this acceptance-plus-condition stance: bending succeeds at the level of the sentence, but at the level of the institution it leaves the structure that produced the situation untouched.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C: surface match — B casts Ngugi as disputing the empirical claim that English can be inflected to carry Igbo rhythms and proverbs. Text 2 does not take this line; it grants the linguistic point and confines its objection to the institutional consequences. B confuses an institutional dispute with a linguistic one.\n- Choice A: inverse — C treats Ngugi as claiming Achebe's program has \"already been disproven\" by reception data. Text 2 makes no such claim; Ngugi's argument is normative and infrastructural (about which audience the work primarily addresses and which institutional structures it sustains), not a verdict that the global audience never materialized.\n- Choice D: scope shift — D reframes the dispute as a theoretical-incoherence problem about whether grammar from one tradition can carry imagery from another. Nothing in Text 2 raises a grammar-and-imagery incompatibility; the disagreement is institutional and political.",
