          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "When the pharmacologist Frances Oldham Kelsey joined the U.S. Food and Drug Administration as a medical officer in 1960, one of her first assignments was the review of an application from the William S. Merrell Company to market the sedative thalidomide in the United States under the trade name Kevadon. The drug was already widely sold in West Germany and the United Kingdom and was generally regarded as safe enough to be sold over the counter. Kelsey, however, treated the data submitted with the application as incomplete: pharmacokinetic studies in pregnant animals were missing, peripheral nerve effects reported in adult patients had not been adequately followed up, and several of the human safety studies relied on uncontrolled physician reports rather than designed trials. Her repeated requests for additional data delayed approval long enough that, when the connection between thalidomide and severe birth defects was made public in late 1961, the drug had not yet been approved for general sale in the United States.",
