          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The passage walks through one episode in Kelsey's regulatory work: she received the thalidomide application in 1960, judged the submitted data incomplete on three specific grounds (missing pharmacokinetic studies in pregnant animals, inadequately followed-up peripheral nerve effects, reliance on uncontrolled physician reports), and through repeated requests for additional data delayed approval long enough that, when the link to severe birth defects became public in late 1961, the drug had not yet been cleared for general U.S. sale. Choice B names the action (the careful review and the specific grounds), the consequence (the delay), and the outcome (no general U.S. approval at the moment the harm became public).\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: scope shift — B promotes one detail from the passage's list of grounds (Kelsey's concern that several of the human safety studies were based on uncontrolled physician reports) into a regulatory reform applied across all new drug applications. The passage describes one application; it does not say Kelsey introduced designed trials as a general rule for the early-1960s FDA.\n- Choice D: inverse — C contradicts the passage. The closing sentence states that the drug had not yet been approved for general sale at the moment the birth-defect connection became public; C asserts the opposite (that thalidomide \"had already been approved for general sale\") and then attributes the withdrawal to the manufacturer.\n- Choice C: surface match — pharmacokinetic studies in pregnant animals are mentioned in the passage as one of three specific data gaps Kelsey flagged, so the noun phrase has a surface anchor in the text. But the passage does not generalize to today's regulatory review of sedatives; treating one item from one application's data list as the single most important consideration in the contemporary FDA process overreads the passage.",
