          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Aerospace engineer Aprille Ericsson, who completed a doctorate at Howard University and spent much of her career at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center, has worked on attitude-control systems for spacecraft — the hardware and software that keep an orbiting satellite pointed where its instruments need to look. In a series of contributions to NASA technical reports across the 1990s and 2000s, Ericsson focused on a recurring engineering tension: spacecraft pointing precision improves when reaction wheels and thrusters fire more frequently and aggressively, but those very corrections introduce small mechanical disturbances that can blur exactly the kind of long-exposure imaging the precision is meant to enable. Her papers examine combinations of sensors, control algorithms, and reaction-wheel timing that limit the disturbance budget while still meeting the pointing tolerance required by particular instruments. The contribution, on her account, is to make the trade-off explicit and tunable rather than buried inside default control settings inherited from earlier missions.",
