          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises: (1) most of the unique taxonomic knowledge in small languages sits in the fine-grained vocabulary layer (medicinal plants, distinctions among related species, specific terms for ice and wind); and (2) the youngest speakers typically lose that layer first, even when they remain conversational in the language overall. Together the premises imply that conversational fluency alone is not a reliable indicator that the unique taxonomic knowledge is being transmitted; the relevant indicator must directly track the fine-grained vocabulary itself. Choice C states exactly that minimum, licensed conclusion.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: scope shift — B leaps from the premises to a sweeping prescriptive conclusion (abandon intergenerational transmission once the fine-grained vocabulary begins to decline). The premises identify a measurement problem with conversational fluency, not a justification for giving up on transmission; B confuses a diagnosis with a counsel of despair.\n- Choice B: inverse — C reverses the passage's claim. The setup explicitly states that communities cannot rely on conversational fluency alone as a measure of language health; C asserts the opposite — that conversational fluency is a sufficient proxy — which the premises directly forbid.\n- Choice D: surface match — D picks up the topical noun phrase \"wider regional or national language\" from the passage and proposes that it serve as the primary repository for local plant and animal taxonomy. The passage explicitly states that the unique taxonomic knowledge is not preserved in those wider languages; D inverts the premise that the wider languages do not carry the layer in question.",
