          "explanation": "The correct answer is B, \"Lise Meitner went on.\" The introductory participial phrase \"Trained as a physicist in Berlin and forced to leave Germany in 1938\" must modify the subject of the main clause that follows. Standard English requires the noun being modified by such a phrase to be a person who was trained and who was forced to leave: \"Trained as a physicist in Berlin and forced to leave Germany in 1938, Lise Meitner went on…\" The remainder of the main clause — \"to use her experimental notes — sent to Sweden ahead of her — to recognize, in correspondence with Otto Hahn that December, that the unusual barium signature he had measured after bombarding uranium with neutrons indicated nuclear fission\" — completes the action she takes after the events the introductory phrase describes. Choice B is the only option among the four whose subject can be trained, can be forced to leave Germany, and can in turn use experimental notes to recognize a phenomenon.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D (\"the experimental notes belonging to Lise Meitner went on\"): inverse — this option produces a dangling-modifier error. The introductory phrase says someone was trained as a physicist and forced to leave Germany; experimental notes cannot be trained or forced to leave Germany. The subject of the main clause must be a person who can take those actions.\n- Choice A (\"it was Lise Meitner's experimental notes that went on\"): scope shift — a different SEC error. The cleft construction (\"it was X that\") makes the experimental notes the grammatical focus and again leaves the introductory phrase modifying notes rather than a person; the dangling-modifier problem persists in a slightly more disguised form, and the cleft structure is also redundant for a sentence whose subject can be named directly.\n- Choice C (\"Lise Meitner's experimental notes went on\"): surface match — this option places \"Lise Meitner\" inside a possessive that cannot serve as the subject the modifier requires. The subject of the main clause is \"experimental notes,\" not Lise Meitner; \"experimental notes\" cannot be trained as a physicist or forced to leave Germany, so the same dangling-modifier error appears in a possessive disguise.",
